+# Product Requirements Document (PRD)
+
+**Project**: Pulseboard  
+**Version**: 1.0.0  
+**Date**: 2026-08-10  
+**Status**: Planning  
+**Scale**: Level 2
+
+---
+
+## 📊 Information Sources
+
+**Traceability**:
+
+- `project.md` — pilot goal, non-goals, and success targets.
+- `product-contract.md` — PRM-001 through PRM-004.
+- `context.md` — two engineers, four-week pilot, Python API, browser UI, and an undecided reminder provider.
+- `evidence-contract.md` — required end-to-end, isolation, and facilitator-LARP evidence.
+
+**Unavailable optional foundation**: `architecture.md`, `ux-strategy.md`, `domain-model.md`, `design-system.md`, and `constitution.md` were not supplied. This plan uses the reversible implementation assumptions in Appendix B; the later epic-planning phase must replace them if an architecture decision is introduced.
+
+No market research or brownfield artifacts were supplied or needed to plan the bounded pilot.
+
+---
+
+## Executive Summary
+
+Pulseboard helps meeting facilitators turn an uploaded transcript into reviewed action items. A facilitator must explicitly confirm every item’s text, owner, and due date before Pulseboard schedules a reminder. The four-week pilot measures whether five teams review at least 80% of proposals and reduce unowned overdue actions by 30%.
+
+## Product Vision & Strategy
+
+### Vision Statement
+
+Meeting follow-through is trustworthy when actions are proposed quickly but never acted on autonomously. Pulseboard makes the review step the product, rather than hiding it behind automation.
+
+### Strategic Objectives
+
+1. **Make action capture faster**: convert an uploaded transcript into actionable proposals for facilitator review.
+2. **Keep facilitators in control**: make text, owner, and due date explicit confirmation gates.
+3. **Prove pilot value safely**: send one reminder through a configurable provider while keeping workspace data isolated.
+
+### Success Definition
+
+- **Pilot success**: five teams use the pilot in four weeks.
+- **User success**: at least 80% of proposed actions are reviewed.
+- **Outcome success**: 30% fewer unowned overdue actions versus each pilot team’s baseline.
+
+## Market Analysis
+
+### Target Market
+
+The initial market is the five pilot teams. No TAM, SAM, SOM, or competitor claims are asserted because the supplied foundation contains no market evidence.
+
+### Competitive Landscape
+
+| Alternative | Strength | Limitation for this pilot | Pulseboard distinction |
+|-------------|----------|---------------------------|------------------------|
+| Manual meeting notes | Familiar and controllable | Actions can be missed or remain unowned | Proposes actions from a transcript, then requires review |
+| Calendar/task tools | Can send reminders | Do not establish that a facilitator approved the meeting action | Confirmation is required before a reminder is scheduled |
+
+### Market Opportunity
+
+The pilot tests a narrow, observable workflow before any market-scale claim: upload, review, confirm, deliver one reminder, and inspect its status.
+
+## User Analysis
+
+### User Personas
+
+**Meeting Facilitator**
+
+- **Goals**: turn discussion into clear owned actions without retyping notes.
+- **Pain Points**: actions are missed, have ambiguous owners or dates, and reminders can be sent too early.
+- **Success Looks Like**: reviewing a short set of proposals, correcting them where needed, and knowing exactly what reminder was delivered.
+
+**Action Owner**
+
+- **Goals**: receive a clear reminder for a confirmed action.
+- **Pain Points**: vague or unowned follow-up from meetings.
+- **Success Looks Like**: a single reminder names a clear action and due date.
+
+### User Journey Map: Reviewed meeting follow-through
+
+1. **Awareness**: a facilitator sees that meeting actions are slipping through ordinary notes.
+2. **Consideration**: the facilitator chooses a transcript to upload.
+3. **Onboarding**: the facilitator uploads it and sees proposed actions.
+4. **Value realization**: the facilitator confirms text, owner, and due date; Pulseboard schedules the reminder.
+5. **Advocacy**: the facilitator can point to delivery status and fewer unowned overdue actions.
+
+## Functional Requirements
+
+### Core Features
+
+#### Reviewed action workflow
+
+**Transcript action proposals**
+
+- **Description**: a facilitator uploads a transcript and receives proposed action items.
+- **User Stories**:
+  - As a facilitator, I want to upload a transcript so that I can review proposed actions without manually extracting each one.
+- **Acceptance Criteria**:
+  - A workspace-scoped upload produces action proposals linked to that transcript.
+  - A proposal is visibly distinguishable from a confirmed action.
+- **Priority**: P0 (PRM-001)
+
+**Explicit confirmation**
+
+- **Description**: confirmation requires action text, owner, and due date.
+- **User Stories**:
+  - As a facilitator, I want to correct and confirm an action so that no incomplete action is scheduled.
+- **Acceptance Criteria**:
+  - The system cannot schedule an action without facilitator confirmation of all three fields.
+  - Confirmed actions retain their approved values.
+- **Priority**: P0 (PRM-002)
+
+#### Workspace access and isolation
+
+**Workspace-scoped data**
+
+- **Description**: transcripts and actions are visible only in their owning workspace.
+- **Acceptance Criteria**:
+  - A real request made as a principal from workspace B cannot read workspace A’s transcripts or actions.
+- **Priority**: P0 (PRM-004)
+
+#### Reminder delivery
+
+**Configurable reminder provider**
+
+- **Description**: a confirmed action sends one reminder through the selected provider and displays its delivery status.
+- **Acceptance Criteria**:
+  - The provider is configurable without changing the reviewed-action workflow.
+  - Each attempted delivery records a user-visible status.
+- **Priority**: P0 (PRM-003)
+
+### Platform Requirements
+
+- **Web**: browser UI for facilitators; responsive enough for common laptop and tablet browser use during the pilot.
+- **Mobile**: no native mobile app is in pilot scope.
+- **Desktop**: no native desktop app is in pilot scope.
+
+### Integration Requirements
+
+| System | Type | Purpose | Priority |
+|--------|------|---------|----------|
+| Reminder provider (TBD) | Provider adapter/API | Deliver one configured reminder and receive delivery result | P0 |
+
+## Non-Functional Requirements
+
+### Performance
+
+- Transcript upload and proposal generation must give the facilitator a clear progress or completion state; no numeric latency target is supplied.
+- Pilot capacity is five teams; no throughput or scale claim is made beyond that bounded use.
+
+### Reliability
+
+- A reminder delivery attempt must persist a status that the facilitator can inspect.
+- Failed provider delivery must be represented as a status, not presented as a successful reminder.
+
+### Security
+
+- Workspace scoping is mandatory for transcripts and actions.
+- Isolation evidence must use a negative probe through the real request role.
+- No regulated health or payment data is in scope.
+
+### Usability
+
+- The confirmation gate must clearly identify missing text, owner, or due date.
+- A facilitator LARP is required for user-facing quality evidence.
+
+### Scalability
+
+- The pilot is deliberately limited to five teams; broader growth is a revisit trigger, not a current requirement.
+
+## Design Principles
+
+### User Experience Principles
+
+1. **Propose, then confirm**: AI-assisted extraction may suggest an action, but the facilitator owns the decision to schedule it.
+2. **Make state legible**: distinguish proposed, confirmed, scheduled, delivered, and failed states.
+3. **Keep the workflow short**: the primary job is upload → review → confirm → inspect delivery.
+
+### Technical Principles
+
+1. **Workspace isolation at the request boundary**: scope reads and writes by the acting workspace rather than filtering only in the browser.
+2. **Provider behind a boundary**: reminder delivery is an adapter so the unselected provider remains a reversible choice.
+3. **Evidence follows the real path**: prove the complete upload-to-delivery path and the cross-workspace negative probe.
+
+## Constraints & Assumptions
+
+### Business Constraints
+
+- **Timeline**: four-week pilot.
+- **Resources**: two engineers.
+- **Pilot population**: five teams.
+- **Legal**: no regulated health or payment data.
+
+### Technical Constraints
+
+- **Application shape**: Python API and browser UI.
+- **Reminder provider**: not selected at planning time.
+- **Non-goals**: meeting recording, calendar replacement, and autonomous outbound messaging.
+
+### Assumptions
+
+1. **A workspace principal will be available to the API**: required to enforce PRM-004; revisit if the chosen authentication model cannot supply it.
+2. **Transcripts can be uploaded as pilot inputs**: revisit if file handling, size, or retention constraints emerge.
+3. **A provider can report a delivery result**: revisit if the selected provider has only fire-and-forget semantics.
+
+## Release Strategy
+
+### MVP Scope
+
+The MVP is the single facilitator workflow: upload transcript, review proposed actions, explicitly confirm text/owner/due date, deliver one reminder via the configured provider, and inspect its status with workspace isolation enforced.
+
+#### MVP Features
+
+- Reviewed transcript-to-action workflow — delivers PRM-001 and PRM-002.
+- Workspace-scoped access — delivers PRM-004.
+- One configurable reminder delivery with status — delivers PRM-003.
+
+#### MVP Success Criteria
+
+- Five teams can complete the primary workflow during the pilot.
+- At least 80% of proposals are reviewed.
+- Unowned overdue actions decrease by 30% over four weeks.
+
+### Release Phases
+
+**Phase 0: Foundation**
+
+- Features: local test/lint/build path, CI, environment configuration, and pilot-safe error observability.
+- Success: the team can exercise and protect the pilot path before feature work.
+
+**Phase 1: Reviewed actions**
+
+- Features: workspace access, transcript upload, action proposals, and explicit confirmation.
+- Success: a facilitator completes the review gate without a reminder being sent automatically.
+
+**Phase 2: Delivery and pilot proof**
+
+- Features: provider adapter, one reminder delivery, status, and pilot metrics.
+- Success: the end-to-end evidence path is available for the chosen provider.
+
+### Rollout Strategy
+
+- **Beta program**: invite the five pilot teams only.
+- **Feature flags**: use a reversible pilot enablement mechanism if the selected deployment environment supports it.
+- **Rollback plan**: disable reminder dispatch while preserving confirmation and delivery-attempt status for investigation.
+
+## Success Metrics
+
+### Key Performance Indicators
+
+**User and outcome metrics**
+
+- Proposal review rate: at least 80% during the four-week pilot.
+- Unowned overdue actions: 30% reduction from each team’s baseline at pilot end.
+- Active pilot teams: five teams.
+
+**Technical metrics**
+
+- End-to-end path: upload-to-confirmation-to-delivery runtime evidence captured for the selected provider.
+- Isolation: a real cross-workspace negative probe is denied.
+
+### Measurement Plan
+
+- **Analytics**: record proposal created, reviewed, confirmed, delivery attempted, and delivery-status events with workspace-safe aggregation.
+- **Feedback loops**: facilitator LARP during validation plus pilot facilitator feedback during the four weeks.
+- **Review cycle**: review pilot metrics weekly; compare unowned overdue actions at pilot end.
+
+## Risk Analysis
+
+### Risk Matrix
+
+| Risk | Probability | Impact | Mitigation | Owner |
+|------|------------|--------|------------|-------|
+| Proposed action is wrong or incomplete | M | H | Require confirmation of text, owner, and due date | Product/engineering |
+| Reminder provider is delayed or unsuitable | M | H | Use a provider adapter and make delivery status explicit | Engineering |
+| Workspace data leaks across teams | L | H | Enforce request-boundary scoping and run a real negative probe | Engineering |
+| Four-week scope expands | M | H | Keep recording, calendar replacement, and autonomous outreach out of scope | Product/engineering |
+| Facilitators do not find review valuable | M | M | Run facilitator LARP and inspect review-rate metric early | Product |
+
+### Contingency Plans
+
+- **If the provider is not selected in time**: complete E001 and keep dispatch disabled; do not simulate delivery as successful.
+- **If review rate is low after the first week**: inspect the facilitator workflow and prioritize friction reduction before expanding scope.
+
+## Dependencies
+
+### External Dependencies
+
+- **Reminder provider (TBD)**: required only for actual dispatch and delivery status.
+
+### Internal Dependencies
+
+- **E000**: provides the test, CI, environment, and observability baseline.
+- **E001**: supplies confirmed actions and workspace context consumed by E002.
+
+## Appendices
+
+### A. Research Data
+
+No research reports were supplied; this plan makes no external market, pricing, or competitor claims.
+
+### B. Technical Architecture
+
+The supplied foundation specifies only a Python API and browser UI. Planning assumes a workspace-scoped persistence layer, a reviewed-action workflow in the API, and a reminder-provider adapter. These are reversible assumptions, not an architecture lock.
+
+### C. Mockups/Wireframes
+
+No design artifacts were supplied. UI-bearing stories must create their canonical UI/experience artifacts in their later lifecycle phases.
+
+### D. Glossary
+
+- **Proposal**: an action candidate extracted from a transcript, not yet eligible for scheduling.
+- **Confirmed action**: an action for which a facilitator has explicitly confirmed text, owner, and due date.
+- **Delivery status**: the recorded state of one reminder attempt through the configured provider.
+- **Workspace**: the tenant boundary for transcript and action access.
+
